#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:1353
#, no-c-format
msgid "&kword; Technical details"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:1357
#, no-c-format
msgid "Kword &MIME; types"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: secondary
#: index.docbook:1358
#, no-c-format
msgid "&MIME; types"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1360
#, no-c-format
msgid "The &kword; &MIME; type is : application/x-kword"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:1367
#, no-c-format
msgid "Glossary"
msgstr "Glossari"

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1370
#, no-c-format
msgid "Aspect Ratio"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1372
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is the ratio of the measurement of a screen, picture or document "
"horizontally compared to the vertical measurement. All standard computer "
"monitors have the same aspect ratio, regardless of their resolution. Any "
"&kword; document that conforms to the standard screen will look good on any "
"monitor."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1377
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For many images, it is important to maintain the aspect ratio. This prevents "
"distortion to the picture."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1383
#, no-c-format
msgid "Binary code"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1385
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Binary code is the actual instructions for the computer. So if we refer to "
"<emphasis>binaries</emphasis> we mean the executable &kword; program. While "
"computers have no difficulties reading binary files, they are not easily "
"understood by people. Compare this distribution method to <glossterm linkend="
"\"defsourcecode\">Source Code</glossterm>. For more information on compiling "
"&kword;, see the section entitled <link linkend=\"installation"
"\">Installation</link>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1396
#, no-c-format
msgid "Bitmap Image"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1398
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Bit mapped images are composed of individual dots. This type of file is very "
"good for photographs and complex drawings. The downside of using bitmap "
"images, is that when you change the size of the image on the page, there is "
"a loss of detail. The file names of such pictures often end in "
"<emphasis>jpeg</emphasis>, <emphasis>png</emphasis> or <emphasis>gif</"
"emphasis>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1408
#, no-c-format
msgid "Cells"
msgstr "Cellulas"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1410
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Tables are made up of rows and columns of cells. Each cell is defined by a "
"combination of the row and column of a heading."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1416
#, no-c-format
msgid "Clipboard"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1418
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The clipboard is a temporary storage area in memory. Whenever you use the "
"<guimenuitem>Cut</guimenuitem> or <guimenuitem>Copy</guimenuitem> command, "
"you are placing the object that was selected into this memory location. Then "
"when you use the <guimenuitem>Paste</guimenuitem> command, you insert the "
"information from the clipboard into the document. For more information see "
"the section on <link linkend=\"clipboard\">Cut/Copy/Paste</link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1429
#, no-c-format
msgid "Compiling"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1431
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you compile a program, you are converting it from a source file (which "
"is easily edited by a programmer) into a binary file (which the computer "
"uses). Compare this distribution method to <glossterm linkend=\"defbinarycode"
"\">Binary Code</glossterm>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1435
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Compiling programs is not as easy as installing binary versions. Users who "
"are only interested in using &kword; are encouraged to find a binary version "
"of &kword; for their computer. For users who want to learn more about "
"programming, more information on compiling &kword; is available in the "
"section entitled <link linkend=\"installation\">Installation</link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1444
#, no-c-format
msgid "Custom Variables"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1446
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Using custom variables you can store certain values (&eg; numbers or text) "
"for later use in your document or in a script. If you want to find out more "
"about custom variables, see <link linkend=\"variables\">Document Variables</"
"link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1454
#, no-c-format
msgid "<glossterm>DEB</glossterm>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1456
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is a binary file format that is used by Debian and Debian-based "
"distributions. This will be the suffix of a file specifically for these "
"distributions. An example would be <filename>koffice-1.2.deb</filename>. For "
"more information on installing these files, refer to the <ulink url=\"http://"
"www.debian.org\">Debian Web Site</ulink>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1467
#, no-c-format
msgid "Dialog Box"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1469
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A dialog box is a small window that appears on top of your working document. "
"This window usually has questions, information or configuration options "
"related to the task you are performing. When you are finished with the "
"dialog box, it will disappear and return you to your document (possibly "
"after making some changes to the document)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: acronym
#: index.docbook:1479
#, no-c-format
msgid "<acronym>DTP</acronym>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1481
#, no-c-format
msgid "Stands for <emphasis>DeskTop Publishing</emphasis>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: acronym
#: index.docbook:1486
#, no-c-format
msgid "<acronym>FAQ</acronym>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1488
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Stands for <emphasis>Frequently Asked Questions</emphasis> and normally "
"means a document, where questions that arise many times are answered. If you "
"have a question to the developers of &koffice;, you should always have a "
"look at the <acronym>FAQ</acronym> first; you can find the latest version "
"<ulink url=\"http://koffice.kde.org/faq\">here</ulink>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1498
#, no-c-format
msgid "File Mask"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1500
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A file mask can be thought of as a strainer for you. On the average "
"computer, there are several thousand files. These files are sorted into sub-"
"directories, but it is not uncommon for many users to have 100's of data "
"files in a single sub-directory."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1504
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Fortunately for us, most applications use a suffix to their filenames. By "
"applying a file mask, &kword; will only show you the files which are not "
"filtered out by the mask. (That is to say &kword; only shows the files that "
"fit through the strainer). This can be helpful if you have many files from "
"many different applications. The file mask is incorporated in the "
"<guilabel>Filter:</guilabel> line of the <guilabel>Save Document</guilabel> "
"and <guilabel>Open Document</guilabel> dialog boxes."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1511
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"As an example. If you use a file mask for &kword; files, the file mask will "
"try to filter out all files that are not for &kword;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1517
#, no-c-format
msgid "Filter"
msgstr "Filtre"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1519
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A filter takes a document file from one program (&eg; &Microsoft; "
"<application>Word</application>), and <emphasis>filters</emphasis> out the "
"text and formatting information and converts that information into a &kword; "
"document. Filters are used to read and write files for other programs. More "
"information on the filters included with &kword; is available in the section "
"entitled <link linkend=\"filters\">Import/Export Filters</link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1529
#, no-c-format
msgid "Footer"
msgstr "Pè"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1531
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The footer of a page is an area below the normal text area. Often it "
"contains the page numbering and maybe some additional information. The "
"contents of the footer are normally the same for most pages, and changing "
"the footer on one page will change all other pages as well. See <glossterm "
"linkend=\"defheader\">Header</glossterm>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1540
#, no-c-format
msgid "Frame"
msgstr "Encastre"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1542
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Nearly everything in &kword; is in a frame. Text is always in a text frame. "
"Pictures are in picture frames. Parts are in part frames. A frame is "
"basically just a rectangle that can contain some part of your document. "
"Frames can be moved, resized, deleted etc."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1550
#, no-c-format
msgid "Frameset"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1552
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A frame-set is a group of frames which are connected together. If you are "
"writing text that does not fit into the first frame of a frame-set, it will "
"continue in the next frame belonging to that frame-set."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1559
#, no-c-format
msgid "&FTP;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1561
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&FTP; is the <emphasis>File Transfer Protocol</emphasis>; it is an Internet "
"protocol that allows you to retrieve files from so-called &FTP; servers. If "
"you want to download &koffice; from the Internet, you will probably use "
"&FTP;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1569
#, no-c-format
msgid "Hard Frame Break"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1571
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Normally, &kword; automatically adjusts text so it fits into a text frame. "
"By inserting a hard frame break you can force &kword; to always start the "
"next frame in the frameset with the text that follows the break."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1579
#, no-c-format
msgid "Hanging indention"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1581
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A paragraph where the first line of the paragraph extends outwards to the "
"left compared with other lines in the paragraph."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1589
#, no-c-format
msgid "Header"
msgstr "Encap"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1591
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The header of a page is an area above the normal text area. Often it "
"contains the page numbering and maybe some additional information. The "
"contents of the header normally are the same for most pages, and changing "
"the header on one page will change all other pages as well. See <glossterm "
"linkend=\"deffooter\">Footer</glossterm>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: acronym
#: index.docbook:1600
#, no-c-format
msgid "<acronym>&HTML;</acronym>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1602
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Stands for <emphasis>HyperText Markup Language</emphasis>. Most web pages on "
"the Internet are written in &HTML;. &kword; can read and write &HTML; "
"documents for publishing on the World Wide Web."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1611
#, no-c-format
msgid "<glossterm>Hue</glossterm>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1613
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Hue is a more technically correct term for what we generally refer to as "
"<emphasis>color</emphasis>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1614
#, no-c-format
msgid "Examples of hues include red, green, blue and purple."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1619
#, no-c-format
msgid "Inline frame"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1621
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"An inline frame is a special frame type in &kword;. Inline frames are "
"associated with a position in a text frame. &kword; will keep the inline "
"frame near the specified text. You will not be able to determine where "
"exactly on a page the frame will appear, but it will be located close to the "
"specified text."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1624
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you insert or delete text in front of the specified text, the inline "
"image will move up or down the page to follow the specified text"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1626
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Inline frames are very useful when they contain a picture, graph or figure. "
"Simply place the inline frame within the text which describes the object, "
"and &kword; will make sure both the text and the inline frame are always "
"near each other."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1632
#, no-c-format
msgid "&kde;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1634
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Stands for the <emphasis>K Desktop Environment</emphasis>. Part of &kde; is "
"required for &kword; to operate. The K Desktop Environment is a user "
"interface which allows users to manipulate files and operate programs "
"graphically. For more information, please visit <ulink url=\"http://www.kde."
"org\">www.kde.org</ulink>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1643
#, no-c-format
msgid "Key Binding"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1645
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"All of the features of &kword; are available through the menubar. You will "
"find, however, that there are certain features of &kword; that you use on a "
"regular basis. You can <emphasis>bind</emphasis> a certain key combination "
"to that function. Once this combination is bound to the function, you can "
"use it as a shortcut to the function. &kword; comes with several <link "
"linkend=\"keys\">predefined key-bindings</link>. For more information on "
"changing the default key-bindings, click <link linkend=\"options-keybindings"
"\">here</link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1657
#, no-c-format
msgid "Landscape"
msgstr "Païsatge"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1659
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you have a standard sheet of paper, you can either orient your document "
"with the long side vertically or horizontally. When the horizontal dimension "
"is greater than the vertical, this is termed Landscape."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1666
#, no-c-format
msgid "Compare with <glossterm linkend=\"defportrait\">Portrait</glossterm>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1672
#, no-c-format
msgid "Menubar"
msgstr "Barra de menuts"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1674
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The menubar is located at the top of the &kword; screen. You can use it to "
"access all features of &kword;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1676
#, no-c-format
msgid "Menubar:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1683
#, no-c-format
msgid "Portrait"
msgstr "Contrast"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1685
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you have a standard sheet of paper, you can either orient your document "
"with the long side vertically or horizontally. When the vertical dimension "
"is greater than the horizontal, this is termed Portrait."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1692
#, no-c-format
msgid "Compare with <glossterm linkend=\"deflandscape\">Landscape</glossterm>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: acronym
#: index.docbook:1698
#, no-c-format
msgid "<acronym>RPM</acronym>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1700
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is the binary file format for distributions based on the &RedHat; "
"package manager, a widely used packaging tool for the &Linux; operating "
"system. If you still have to get &koffice; and your system supports "
"<acronym>RPM</acronym> packages, you should get &koffice; packages ending in "
"<literal role=\"extension\">.rpm</literal>. They are very easy to use."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: acronym
#: index.docbook:1710
#, no-c-format
msgid "Saturation"
msgstr "Saturacion"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1712
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Saturation refers to the subjective quantity of a specific hue in a color. "
"Colors with a low saturation appear more white. Colors with high saturation "
"appear more richly colored."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1714
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is a set of four red dots which increase in saturation from left to "
"right."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1721
#, no-c-format
msgid "Scaling Pictures"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1723
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Whenever you change the size of a graphics image, you are scaling that "
"image. In &kword; you scale the pictures by changing the shape of the frame "
"which surrounds the graphic."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1730
#, no-c-format
msgid "Source Code"
msgstr "Còdi sorga"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1732
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Source code is the human readable version of an application (such as "
"&kword;). Computers cannot use source code directly. Instead, source code "
"must be <glossterm linkend=\"defcompiling\">compiled</glossterm> into binary "
"code, before use."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1740
#, no-c-format
msgid "<glossterm>TAR</glossterm>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1742
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Tar is a tool used for the archiving of files in so-called <emphasis>tar-"
"files</emphasis> which you recognize by their suffix <literal role="
"\"extension\">.tar</literal>. You can find &koffice; source and binary "
"distributions as <glossterm linkend=\"deftgz\">gzipped tar-files</"
"glossterm>; however, you should not use them if there are special packages "
"for your system and package manager. See <glossterm linkend=\"defrpm\">RPM</"
"glossterm>, <glossterm linkend=\"defdeb\">DEB</glossterm>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literal
#: index.docbook:1753
#, no-c-format
msgid "tar.gz"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1755
#, no-c-format
msgid "See <glossterm linkend=\"deftgz\"><acronym>TGZ</acronym></glossterm>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: acronym
#: index.docbook:1760
#, no-c-format
msgid "<acronym>TGZ</acronym>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1762
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Files ending in <literal role=\"extension\">.tar.gz</literal> or <literal "
"role=\"extension\">.tgz</literal> are <glossterm linkend=\"deftar\">tar-"
"files</glossterm> compressed with the <command>gzip</command> program. This "
"makes the tar files smaller and quicker to download. You can find &koffice; "
"source and binary distributions in this format; however, you should not use "
"them if there are special packages for your system and package manager. See "
"<glossterm linkend=\"defrpm\">RPM</glossterm>, <glossterm linkend=\"defdeb"
"\">DEB</glossterm>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1774
#, no-c-format
msgid "Toolbar"
msgstr "Barra d'espleches"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1776
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A toolbar is a line of buttons which are shortcuts to more of the commonly "
"used features of &kword;. More information on toolbars can be found <link "
"linkend=\"toolbars\">here</link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1779
#, no-c-format
msgid "Example Toolbar:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: glossterm
#: index.docbook:1786
#, no-c-format
msgid "&URL;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1788
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&URL; is an abbreviation for Universal Resource Locater. A universal "
"resource locater is the technical term for what is commonly referred to as a "
"websites address."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1790
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Examples of &URL;s include <ulink url=\"http://www.koffice.org\">http://www."
"koffice.org</ulink> and <ulink url=\"http://www.kde.org\">http://www.kde."
"org</ulink>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: acronym
#: index.docbook:1796
#, no-c-format
msgid "Value (color)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1798
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Color value refers to how bright or dark a color is. Colors with low value "
"are more black in appearance. Colors with high value are more richly colored."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1800
